1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,004
Imagine it is 11:30 at night in Seoul.

2
00:00:03,004 --> 00:00:08,695
You open an app and order steaming
noodle soup and crispy fried chicken.

3
00:00:08,695 --> 00:00:11,857
Thirty minutes later, your phone chimes.

4
00:00:11,857 --> 00:00:17,074
There is no awkward knock, no cash,
and zero expectation of a tip.

5
00:00:17,074 --> 00:00:21,264
You open your front door,
pick up your meal, and eat.

6
00:00:21,714 --> 00:00:26,050
Rewind a couple of decades,
and the scene gets even wilder.

7
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:31,489
Instead of plastic tubs, your food
arrived in heavy, real porcelain bowls.

8
00:00:31,489 --> 00:00:36,708
When you finished eating, you did
not wash them or throw anything away.

9
00:00:36,708 --> 00:00:42,000
You stacked the dirty ceramic bowls
outside your door and went to sleep.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,410
By morning, the restaurant
picked them up to wash and reuse.

11
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:52,201
In South Korea, food delivery is
not treated as a weekend splurge.

12
00:00:52,201 --> 00:00:57,785
It is an everyday urban utility,
like running water or fast internet.

13
00:00:57,785 --> 00:01:03,935
In many Western cities, service fees
and a 20% tip can double a meal's cost.

14
00:01:03,935 --> 00:01:09,276
In Korea, tipping simply does not
exist in the delivery ecosystem.

15
00:01:09,726 --> 00:01:15,198
When you pay in the app, the price
on the screen is the final price.

16
00:01:15,198 --> 00:01:20,830
Delivery fees are modest, waived
with passes, or folded into minimums.

17
00:01:20,830 --> 00:01:26,785
Single-person households, students,
and families all tap the same network.

18
00:01:26,785 --> 00:01:31,854
Delivery is treated as an extension
of the dining table itself.

19
00:01:32,304 --> 00:01:37,578
Long before smartphones, Korea
already had a deep delivery habit.

20
00:01:37,578 --> 00:01:44,232
Joseon-era records mention nobles ordering
cold naengmyeon noodles to their homes.

21
00:01:44,232 --> 00:01:50,318
A 1925 text called Haedong Jukji
describes hyojonggaeng, or dawn-bell soup.

22
00:01:50,318 --> 00:01:56,322
Pots of soup were wrapped thick in
cotton at night and carried into Seoul.

23
00:01:56,322 --> 00:02:01,272
They arrived steaming hot just
as the 4:00 AM dawn bell rang.

24
00:02:01,722 --> 00:02:07,163
By the mid-twentieth century, that
tradition gained an iconic symbol:

25
00:02:07,163 --> 00:02:11,895
the cheolgabang, a rectangular
silver aluminum delivery box.

26
00:02:11,895 --> 00:02:18,282
Neighborhood Chinese restaurants balanced
hot jajangmyeon and sweet-and-sour pork

27
00:02:18,282 --> 00:02:23,644
inside tiered metal shelves on the
back of bicycles and motorcycles.

28
00:02:23,644 --> 00:02:29,322
Leaving empty ceramic bowls outside
your door was the unspoken contract.

29
00:02:29,772 --> 00:02:34,737
Korea's delivery speed is also
built into its urban geometry.

30
00:02:34,737 --> 00:02:38,318
High-rise apartment
complexes, called danji,

31
00:02:38,318 --> 00:02:41,981
pack thousands of homes
into numbered towers.

32
00:02:41,981 --> 00:02:47,759
Every building has a clear number,
a shared keypad, and fast elevators.

33
00:02:47,759 --> 00:02:53,457
One rider on a scooter can drop off
three meals in under five minutes.

34
00:02:53,457 --> 00:02:59,724
Street-level shops beneath the towers
shrink the distance from stove to sofa.

35
00:03:00,174 --> 00:03:04,953
Even without a street address,
Korea's delivery network adapts.

36
00:03:04,953 --> 00:03:10,036
On warm evenings, thousands spread
picnic mats across public parks.

37
00:03:10,036 --> 00:03:14,361
There are no house numbers on
the grass and no mailboxes.

38
00:03:14,361 --> 00:03:19,671
Yet everywhere you look, people are
unwrapping freshly delivered fried

39
00:03:19,671 --> 00:03:24,678
chicken, hot hamburgers, and fries
right on their picnic blankets.

40
00:03:25,128 --> 00:03:30,634
To make park delivery effortless,
Seoul built official infrastructure.

41
00:03:30,634 --> 00:03:34,174
The Seoul Metropolitan
Government operates 17

42
00:03:34,174 --> 00:03:37,871
designated Delivery Zones
across Hangang parks.

43
00:03:37,871 --> 00:03:43,219
From Yeouido and Banpo to Ttukseom
and Mangwon, riders pull right up

44
00:03:43,219 --> 00:03:48,096
to marked pickup points and hand
over dinner beside the river.

45
00:03:48,546 --> 00:03:51,605
And it does not even
stop at the shoreline.

46
00:03:51,605 --> 00:03:56,727
In Korea, people have famously ordered
steaming bowls of jajangmyeon and

47
00:03:56,727 --> 00:04:01,422
spicy seafood jjamppong straight
onto boats anchored on the water.

48
00:04:01,422 --> 00:04:07,184
Whether at a river dock or from a supply
boat pulling alongside a fishing vessel,

49
00:04:07,184 --> 00:04:12,306
if you can drop a pin where you are
floating, hot noodles will find you.

50
00:04:12,756 --> 00:04:20,599
Official 2025 statistics show just how
massive this daily habit has become.

51
00:04:20,599 --> 00:04:28,337
Total online shopping reached 272
trillion won, with 77% on mobile phones.

52
00:04:28,337 --> 00:04:36,180
Food delivery alone accounted for 15.3%
— over 41 trillion won in one year.

53
00:04:36,630 --> 00:04:41,936
Delivery is no longer limited to
pizza, chicken, and Chinese noodles.

54
00:04:41,936 --> 00:04:47,473
People routinely order a single iced
americano, a handmade burger combo,

55
00:04:47,473 --> 00:04:52,779
shaved ice packed with cold packs,
or fresh sashimi over crushed ice.

56
00:04:52,779 --> 00:04:58,854
Even at one or two in the morning, dozens
of neighborhood kitchens remain open.

57
00:04:59,304 --> 00:05:04,361
The same obsession with speed
transformed grocery shopping too.

58
00:05:04,361 --> 00:05:08,133
With saebyeok baesong —
dawn delivery — you can

59
00:05:08,133 --> 00:05:11,424
order milk, eggs, and greens at 11:00 PM.

60
00:05:11,424 --> 00:05:16,481
While you sleep, insulated chilled
boxes move through automated

61
00:05:16,481 --> 00:05:20,976
hubs and arrive quietly at
your doorstep before 7:00 AM.

62
00:05:21,426 --> 00:05:26,728
To tackle single-use plastic waste,
Korea brought back its own past.

63
00:05:26,728 --> 00:05:31,251
In Seoul, apps let you toggle
a reusable container option.

64
00:05:31,251 --> 00:05:36,943
After eating, you scan a QR code on
the return bag and leave it outside —

65
00:05:36,943 --> 00:05:41,778
or drop it at official return
bins right inside Hangang parks.

66
00:05:42,228 --> 00:05:47,788
From cotton-wrapped dawn soup to silver
metal boxes, park pickup zones, and

67
00:05:47,788 --> 00:05:52,532
noodles delivered onto boats, the
tools have changed completely.

68
00:05:52,532 --> 00:05:55,201
Yet the promise has stayed the same.

69
00:05:55,201 --> 00:05:58,092
Wherever you are — on the 20th floor or

70
00:05:58,092 --> 00:06:01,428
floating on the river
— dinner is on the way.

71
00:06:01,878 --> 00:06:03,774
This is Only in Korea.
